### 🔵 Duty-lane signer set trusts `Validator.active` instead of a bond, so a validator with no stake keeps the zero-fee lane

**Severity:** LOW · Deep review

**TL;DR:** `duty_lane_signers` admits consensus validators on `active && !slashed && !jailed` and never checks stake; `active` survives an unstake to 1 atom and comes back on `Resume` after a full unstake, so the free lane needs no bond.

**Impact:** An account with no bonded stake at all can hold the zero-fee duty lane, filling its byte budget and per-window slot with unpaid duty transactions and competing with the bonded validators the lane exists for.

**Root cause:**
- The new gate treats `Validator.active` as proof that a bond stands behind the account, but that flag records eligibility when it was last set and nothing recomputes it when stake leaves, so the bond the doc comment relies on is never checked.
- `Unstake` clears `active` only when the stake reaches exactly zero (`src/execution/executor.rs:865-867`), so unstaking down to 1 atom leaves the validator inside `duty_lane_signers` (`src/core/account.rs:1851-1855`) even though its bond is far below the class floor.
- The zero-stake case is easier still: the epoch step re-activates a resumed validator on `!slashed && !jailed` alone (`src/core/account.rs:3683-3688`) and `Unstake` refuses only jailed validators, so suspended -&gt; unstake everything -&gt; `Resume` yields `active = true` at zero stake.

```suggestion
        let floor = self.registry.params().min_stake;
        for (address, validator) in &self.validators {
            if validator.active
                && !validator.slashed
                && !validator.jailed
                && validator.stake >= floor
            {
                signers.insert(*address);
            }
        }
```

